@Jamesernator @annevk 
I thought about the serialization steps, but when I read through these lines in serializing an element, I thought all attributes are serialized automatically. Am I missing something here?

> For each attribute that the element has, append a U+0020 SPACE character, the [attribute's serialized name as described below](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#attribute's-serialised-name), a U+003D EQUALS SIGN character (=), a U+0022 QUOTATION MARK character ("), the attribute's value, [escaped as described below](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#escapingString) in attribute mode, and a second U+0022 QUOTATION MARK character (").

I see what you mean. Then here's an open question on serialization if we expect the attribute to be serialized on element using scoped registry I want to verify here:
```
<div customelementregistry><foo><bar></bar></foo></div>

const host = document.querySelector('div');
const registry = new CustomElementRegistry();
registry.initialize(host);
```
In this case, initialize will give `foo` and `bar` scoped registry. When we're serializing `host`, are we expecting to see `customelementregistry` attribute on `foo` and `bar` as well? It feels a bit odd since they didn't have those attribute at the first place.

In Chrome/Safari, the behavior with respect to fragment parsing is inconsistent. These may be bugs but posting here since it may need spec clarification:

From [example](https://codepen.io/editor/Steve-Orvell/pen/01a0d3eb-1461-7f07-a173-f4496ccb4986):

```js
container.innerHTML = `<div customelementregistry></div>`;
// Fails in Safari/Chrome, Passes FF (with flag)
console.assert(container.firstChild.customElementRegistry === null, 'innerHTML: with customelementregistry attr');

container.innerHTML = `<div customelementregistry><x-a></x-a></div>`;
// Passes in Safari/Chrome, Passes FF (with flag)
console.assert(container.firstChild.customElementRegistry === null, 'innerHTML: with customelementregistry attr and ce child');

container.setHTMLUnsafe(`<div customelementregistry></div>`);
// Passes in Safari/Chrome/FF (with flag)
console.assert(container.firstChild.customElementRegistry === null, 'setHTMLUnsafe: with customelementregistry attr');
```
